{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8aded30131d7dc8c", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tSAGE: Switch-Aware EEG-Guided Soft Gating for Target Speaker Extraction\n2\twith In-Trial Switching\n3\tXuefei Wang ID 1 , Ximin Chen ID 1 , Yuting Ding ID 1 , Chunlin Li2 , Fei Chen ID 1,∗∗\n4\t1\n5\t\n6\tDepartment of Electronic and Electrical Engineering, Southern University of Science and\n7\tTechnology, Shenzhen, China\n8\t2\n9\tSchool of Biomedical Engineering, Capital Medical University, Beijing, China\n10\t12431243@mail.sustech.edu.cn, fchen@sustech.edu.cn\n11\t\n12\tarXiv:2608.01623v1 [eess.SP] 3 Aug 2026\n13\t\n14\tAbstract\n15\tEEG-guided target speaker extraction is challenging under intrial auditory attention switching, where neural noise and intrinsic latency can delay or destabilize attention tracking. Conventional methods struggle with dynamic switches and often cause\n16\tdiscontinuities at switching points. Therefore, we propose\n17\tSAGE, a switch-aware EEG-guided soft gating framework that\n18\ttreats in-trial switching as dynamic selection. SAGE generates\n19\ttwo candidate speech streams with a robust separator and uses\n20\tan EEG-guided switch-aware gating module to produce smooth\n21\tfusion weights and suppress transition artifacts. We further integrate latency-compensated alignment and an uncertainty-driven\n22\tconservative strategy to handle latency discrepancies and fluctuating EEG reliability. SAGE outperforms baselines, achieving 8.67 dB SI-SDR and 88.24% STOI while reducing average\n23\tswitching latency to 2.04 s. By coupling neural decoding with\n24\tspeech separation, it enables robust target extraction in dynamic\n25\tscenarios.\n26\tIndex Terms: target speaker extraction, EEG decoding, switchaware gating, neural latency compensation, uncertainty-aware\n27\tstrategy\n28\t\n29\t1. Introduction\n30\tThe cocktail party problem [1] describes the remarkable human\n31\tability to focus on a target talker in complex multi-speaker environments through selective auditory attention. Target speaker\n32\textraction (TSE) aims to address this challenge by leveraging\n33\tauxiliary cues to separate the attended speech signal from a mixture. Electroencephalography (EEG) provides a direct measure\n34\tof a listener’s neural activity and cognitive state, offering neurophysiological evidence of which speaker is being attended [2,3].\n35\tWith millisecond-level temporal resolution, EEG can track the\n36\tdynamics of attention in real time, making it an attractive physiological signal for guiding target speaker extraction.\n37\tDriven by rapid advances in deep learning, the performance\n38\tof TSE has improved substantially [4, 5]. Conventional TSE\n39\tmethods typically rely on enrollment utterances of the target\n40\tspeaker or pre-extracted speaker embeddings, requiring prior\n41\taccess to target speech samples and performing well in fixed\n42\tsingle-target settings [6–9]. To reduce dependence on enrollment speech, researchers have explored alternative auxiliary\n43\tcues, including spatial information for localization-assisted separation [10], visual cues such as lip movements to enhance directional focus and robustness [11], and biologically inspired\n44\tmechanisms that emulate neural processing to improve source\n45\tseparation [12].\n46\t** indicates the corresponding author.\n47\t\n48\tIn recent years, EEG-based TSE has emerged as an active research topic. By decoding auditory attention information\n49\tfrom EEG, neuro-steered target speaker extraction has shown\n50\tstrong potential for practical applications [13]. Pan et al. proposed NeuroHeed+, which significantly improves neuro-guided\n51\tspeaker extraction by jointly modeling auditory attention decoding [14]. Fan et al. introduced DGSD, which applies\n52\tdynamic-graph self-distillation to EEG-based auditory spatial\n53\tattention decoding and improves the accuracy of feature learning [15]. Despite these advances, most existing EEG-guided\n54\tTSE methods adopt a key simplifying assumption that the listener’s attentional focus remains static during an experimental\n55\tsegment. This assumption overlooks a more realistic setting\n56\twhere listeners may spontaneously switch attention between\n57\tspeakers within a single trial, referred to as in-trial switching.\n58\tEEG-guided TSE under in-trial attention switching introduces two central challenges. First, EEG signals are inherently\n59\tnoisy and non-stationary, and are sensitive to environmental\n60\tinterference and inter-subject variability. These properties often degrade the reliability of neural decoding around switching moments, reducing the accuracy of detecting and tracking\n61\tattention shifts [16–18]. Second, neural responses exhibit intrinsic latency, leading to inevitable latency discrepancies between attention-switch labels and true switches. When combined with conventional approaches that rely on fixed speaker\n62\treferences and lack mechanisms to accommodate dynamically\n63\tswitching attention, this mismatch often produces delayed responses and audible discontinuities at switching points, which\n64\tsubstantially harms continuity and intelligibility [19–21]. Although prior studies have attempted to improve EEG decoding\n65\tthrough signal enhancement and feature selection [22, 23], they\n66\tdo not provide dedicated mechanisms tailored to spontaneous\n67\tin-trial attention switching, and cannot fundamentally address\n68\tthe performance degradation caused by endogenous, dynamic\n69\tattention switches.\n70\tTo tackle these issues, we propose SAGE, a switch-aware\n71\tEEG-guided soft-gating framework for target speaker extraction\n72\tduring spontaneous in-trial speaker switching. SAGE makes\n73\tthree main contributions: (1) it uses a front-end separation module to generate two candidate speech streams for dynamic selection; (2) it designs an EEG-guided switch-aware gating module with adaptive temperature scaling to produce smooth fusion\n74\tweights and avoid abrupt switching artifacts; and (3) it incorporates latency-compensated alignment with learnable temporal\n75\tshifts and an uncertainty-driven conservative strategy to handle EEG noise and neural latency. Experiments on spontaneous\n76\tattention-switching datasets show that SAGE improves speech\n77\textraction performance, reduces switching delay, and enhances\n78\tsystem stability compared with strong baselines.\n79\t\n80\t\fFigure 1: The overall framework of the proposed SAGE, switch-aware EEG-guided soft gating for target speaker extraction.\n81\t\n82\t2. Method\n83\tIn this section, we propose a novel dynamic target speaker extraction method based on EEG signals, designed to overcome\n84\tthe limitations of traditional speech separation methods in handling in-trial target speaker switching.\n85\t2.1. Architecture\n86\tAs shown in Fig. 1, the overall framework consists of two main\n87\tparts: speech separation and EEG attention regulation. The\n88\tEEG attention regulation module includes EEG feature extractor, switch-aware soft gating, uncertainty-driven conservative\n89\tstrategy, and latency compensation alignment. The architecture\n90\tutilizes EEG-guided attention switching information and compensates for neural delays, effectively ensuring smooth transitions during target speaker switching. Given a mixed speech\n91\twaveform Stmix ∈ RT ×1 and an EEG sequence e(t) ∈ RC\n92\t(with C channels) sampled and synchronized to the audio timeline, the model outputs the extracted target waveform ŝ(t) that\n93\tfollows the attended speaker even when attention switches.\n94\tThe speech feature extraction module follows the ConvTasNet pipeline [7], which encodes the time-domain mixture\n95\tinto a learnable latent representation and performs separation\n96\tby estimating masks in the latent space. Given the input mixture Stmix , a 1-D convolutional encoder first maps it to an embedding sequence, followed by layer normalization:\n97\t\u0010\n98\t\u0010\n99\t\u0011\u0011\n100\tYt = LayerNorm Conv1Denc Stmix ,\n101\t(1)\n102\t′\n103\t\n104\twhere Yt ∈ RT ×D denotes the encoded features, T ′ is the\n105\tframe length after convolution, and D is the encoder feature dimension. On top of Yt , a temporal convolutional separator composed of stacked dilated 1-D convolutional blocks aggregates\n106\tlong-range context and predicts two mask sequences through\n107\ta 1 × 1 projection with PReLU and sigmoid activations. The\n108\tmasks are applied to the mixture embedding to form two candidate latent streams. Finally, a decoder reconstructs the timedomain candidates to produce two candidate speech streams,\n109\t\n110\ts1 (t), s2 (t), which provide input for speaker selection and synthesis.\n111\tThe EEG attention regulation module generates a timevarying weight function g(t) based on the neural activity in the\n112\tEEG signals, which controls the selection of the final output\n113\tspeech stream. We employ a soft selection mechanism based\n114\ton a time window, aiming to predict the dynamic change of the\n115\ttarget speaker based on the EEG signal. The process is formulated as:\n116\tŝ(t) = g(t) · s1 (t) + (1 − g(t)) · s2 (t),\n117\t\n118\t(2)\n119\t\n120\twhere g(t) ∈ [0, 1] is the dynamic weight generated by the\n121\tneural network based on the EEG signal, indicating the selected\n122\ttarget speaker at time t.\n123\t2.2. Switch-aware Soft Gating\n124\tEEG attention switches are often annotated as discrete change\n125\tpoints, yet the underlying neural evidence evolves continuously,\n126\texhibiting a slowly varying transition rather than an instantaneous jump. Direct hard selection often produces discontinuities or momentary leakage of both speakers around switching\n127\tpoints. Therefore, we propose a switch-aware soft gating mechanism that can introduce smoother transitions by incorporating\n128\tswitch probabilities and temperature scaling.\n129\tWe predict a switch probability psw (t) ∈ [0, 1] reflecting\n130\twhether attention is changing around time t, and an attention\n131\tbias logit α(t) ∈ R indicating which candidate is more likely\n132\tattended. Both are produced from the aligned EEG feature via\n133\tlightweight temporal modeling which enforces temporal consistency. We first compute an intermediate temperature-controlled\n134\tgate gtemp (t):\n135\t\u0013\n136\t\u0012\n137\tα(t)\n138\t, τ (t) = τ0 + λ · psw (t), (3)\n139\tgtemp (t) = σ\n140\tτ (t)\n141\twhere τ0 > 0 is a base temperature and λ ≥ 0 controls\n142\thow much additional smoothing is introduced near potential\n143\t\n144\t\fswitches. Although gtemp (t) already adapts its sharpness via\n145\tτ (t), residual jitter can still arise from EEG noise, especially\n146\tnear switching regions. We therefore obtain the final gating signal g(t) by applying a local diffusion to gtemp (t):\n147\t\u0010\n148\t\u0011\n149\tg(t) = clip (gtemp ∗ wσ(t0 ) )(t) ,\n150\t(4)\n151\twhere ∗ denotes 1D convolution over time, wσ(t0 ) is a normalized smoothing kernel centered at a detected switching center t0\n152\twith learnable width σ(t0 ) > 0, and clip(·) enforces the range\n153\t[0, 1]. The switching centers {t0 } are obtained by thresholding\n154\tpeaks or local maxima in psw (t).\n155\t2.3. Latency Compensation and Uncertainty Strategy\n156\tIn practice, attention-related EEG patterns inevitably lag behind\n157\tannotated switch events, exhibiting intrinsic latency discrepancies that vary significantly across subjects and trials. Without\n158\texplicitly compensating for such delays, the gating signal g(t)\n159\ttends to update after the labeled switch time, increasing wrongspeaker leakage in the transition region. To alleviate this issue,\n160\twe introduce a differentiable time-alignment module that produces an aligned EEG representation e′ (t).\n161\tRather than predicting a single global offset, we formulate\n162\talignment as a local, time-varying soft shift within a bounded\n163\twindow:\n164\tD\n165\tX\n166\te′ (t) =\n167\tw∆ (t) · e(t − ∆),\n168\t(5)\n169\t∆=−D\n170\t\n171\twhere D is the maximum shift, e(t) ∈ RC denotes the Cchannel EEG sample at time t and w∆ (t) is the time-shifting\n172\tweight dynamically predicted by the network. This operation\n173\tcan be interpreted as differentiable temporal resampling over a\n174\tplausible neural-latency window, allowing the model to softly\n175\taggregate EEG evidence from nearby time points instead of\n176\tcommitting to a hard shift. The aligned feature e′ (t) is then\n177\tfed into the gating and switch-prediction network to estimate\n178\tα(t) and psw (t), better synchronizing EEG-driven decisions\n179\twith acoustic changes. However, EEG quality is not constant,\n180\tartifacts, subject fatigue, and transient noise can make EEGderived cues unreliable. Over-confident gating under unreliable\n181\tEEG can lead to persistent wrong-speaker selection. Therefore,\n182\tWe also incorporate an uncertainty-driven conservative strategy that down-weights aggressive switching and encourages\n183\tsmoother gating when EEG is uncertain. We estimate uncertainty by performing K stochastic forward passes with dropout\n184\tenabled in the EEG-related subnetworks, producing a set of gatK\n185\ting samples g (k) (t)k=1 . The uncertainty can be measured as the\n186\ttemporal variance:\n187\t\u0012n\n188\toK \u0013\n189\t(k)\n190\tu(t) = V ar\n191\tg (t)\n192\t,\n193\t(6)\n194\tk=1\n195\t\n196\tand normalized into [0, 1] to obtain a stable control signal. A\n197\thigher u(t) indicates lower confidence in EEG-driven selection. When u(t) is high, we increase the preference for continuity by amplifying a smoothness penalty. We implement this\n198\tby uncertainty-weighted smoothness regularization:\n199\tX\n200\tLsmooth =\n201\tu(t) · R (g(t)) ,\n202\t(7)\n203\tt\n204\t\n205\twhere R(g(t)) is a differentiable smoothness regularizer. This\n206\tdesign prevents the model from making sharp, low-confidence\n207\tdecisions, while still allowing fast transitions when EEG evidence is both strong and reliable.\n208\t\n209\t2.4. Loss Function Design\n210\tDuring training, we optimize the model with a combination of\n211\tspeech reconstruction loss, gating regularization, and switchstructure constraints to improve both extraction quality and temporal stability.\n212\tTo suppress jitter in g(t), we design a switch-aware variant\n213\tthat relaxes the penalty near predicted switching moments:\n214\tX\n215\tLtv-sw =\n216\t(1 − βpsw (t)) , |g(t) − g(t − 1)|,\n217\t(8)\n218\tt\n219\t\n220\twhere β ∈ [0, 1] controls how much smoothing is reduced when\n221\tpsw (t) is high. This encourages stable gating during steady\n222\tattention while allowing faster transitions when the model detects a switch. Besides, We supervise the extracted waveform\n223\tŝ(t) with the ground-truth target speech s(t). Since SI-SDR is\n224\ta maximization metric, we minimize its negative form. The\n225\tfinal training objective combines these losses, including the\n226\tuncertainty-weighted smoothness in Eq. (7), with appropriate\n227\tweights:\n228\tL = −SI-SDR (ŝ(t), s(t)) + γ1 Ltv-sw + γ2 Lsmooth ,\n229\t\n230\t(9)\n231\t\n232\twhere γ1 , γ2 are scalar weights. To ensure stable convergence,\n233\twe adopt a tri-stage training strategy. In stage one, we train the\n234\taudio separator using only audio supervision. In stage two, we\n235\tpartially freeze the separator and jointly train the EEG-related\n236\tmodules. Finally, we perform end-to-end fine-tuning with a\n237\tsmaller learning rate.\n238\t\n239\t3. Experiments\n240\t3.1. Dataset\n241\tIn this study, we use a custom-built dataset for spontaneous\n242\tauditory attention switching based on electroencephalogram\n243\t(EEG) signals [24]. The experimental procedures have been\n244\tapproved by the Ethical Review Board of Southern University\n245\tof Science and Technology (Approval No. 2022DZX003). The\n246\tparticipants consisted of 18 healthy native Mandarin-speaking\n247\tadults (ages 18-27) with normal hearing. During the experiment, EEG data was recorded using 64 electrodes placed according to the international 10-20 system, with a sampling rate\n248\tof 500 Hz and mastoid reference electrodes. The auditory\n249\tstimuli consisted of spatialized mixed speech from one male\n250\tand one female speakers, presented at +90◦ and −90◦ azimuths. Participants were instructed to switch attention spontaneously and signal the switch using a button press. The experiments were conducted in a soundproof room, and the stimuli\n251\twere presented through headphones at a sound pressure level\n252\tof 65 dB SPL. Data preprocessing followed standard procedures, including artifact removal, re-referencing, EEG downsampling to 128 Hz, and application of a 0.1-45 Hz bandpass filter. Additional information and data can be found at:\n253\thttps://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17413336.\n254\t3.2. Experimental Details\n255\tThe proposed model was implemented in Python 3.9 using the\n256\tPyTorch framework, and all experiments were conducted on a\n257\thigh-performance computing system equipped with NVIDIA\n258\tV100 GPUs to accelerate training and evaluation. During training, we employed the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of\n259\t1 × 10−4 , a batch size of 16, and default momentum parameters to optimize model stability and convergence. Each participant’s dataset was split into training, validation, and test sets in a\n260\t\n261\t\f8:1:1 ratio to ensure the model was trained, validated, and evaluated on separate data. The training process spanned multiple\n262\tepochs, and early stopping was employed based on validation\n263\tloss to prevent overfitting. Additionally, regularization techniques such as dropout and weight decay were applied during\n264\ttraining to improve the model’s robustness. During the evaluation phase, we reported scale-invariant signal-to-distortion ratio\n265\t(SI-SDR), short-time objective intelligibility (STOI), switch detection accuracy (ACC) and average switch latency (ASL).\n266\t\n267\t4. Results and Discussion\n268\t4.1. Comparison with Baseline Methods\n269\tTo evaluate our EEG-based target speaker extraction approach,\n270\twe compared it with several baseline models. As shown in Table 1, our method, SAGE, outperformed all baselines across all\n271\tmetrics on the spontaneous attention-switching dataset.\n272\tSAGE achieved 8.67 dB in SI-SDR, surpassing BASEN\n273\t(4.02 dB), NeuroHeed (4.96 dB), NeuroSpex+ (6.21 dB), and\n274\tM3ANet (7.13 dB), demonstrating better interference suppression and speech distortion reduction for higher reconstruction\n275\tquality. In terms of speech intelligibility, SAGE reached a STOI\n276\tscore of 88.24%, outperforming BASEN (74.83%), NeuroHeed\n277\t(79.57%), NeuroSpex+ (82.84%), and M3ANet (84.30%), preserving perceptually important speech components for clearer,\n278\tmore intelligible extraction. SAGE achieved the lowest average switching latency of 2.04 s, outperforming BASEN (2.93\n279\ts), NeuroHeed (2.81 s), NeuroSpex+ (2.56 s), and M3ANet\n280\t(2.37 s). This advantage underscored the effectiveness of the\n281\tlatency-compensated alignment module in alleviating the temporal mismatch between EEG attention labels and actual auditory attention switching, enabling faster and more reliable tracking. Overall, the proposed SAGE framework delivered superior\n282\tperformance over multiple strong baselines, with particularly\n283\tpronounced gains during dynamic target switching.\n284\tTable 1: Comparison with representative EEG-based target\n285\tspeaker extraction baselines on the spontaneous attentionswitching dataset.\n286\tMethods\n287\tBASEN [25]\n288\tNeuroHeed [14]\n289\tNeuroSpex+ [26]\n290\tM3ANet [27]\n291\tSAGE (proposed)\n292\t\n293\tSI SDR (dB)\n294\t4.02\n295\t4.96\n296\t6.21\n297\t7.13\n298\t8.67\n299\t\n300\tSTOI (%)\n301\t74.83\n302\t79.57\n303\t82.84\n304\t84.30\n305\t88.24\n306\t\n307\tASL (s)\n308\t2.93\n309\t2.81\n310\t2.56\n311\t2.37\n312\t2.04\n313\t\n314\t4.2. Ablation Study\n315\tTo evaluate the contributions of each component in the proposed\n316\tmethod, we conducted ablation experiments by removing the\n317\tswitch-aware soft gating, latency-compensated alignment, and\n318\tuncertainty-driven conservative strategy modules, as well as a\n319\tvariant where all modules were removed.\n320\tTable 2 presented the performance of each configuration in\n321\tterms of SI-SDR, STOI, and switch detection accuracy. Removing any module resulted in performance degradation, highlighting the importance of each design choice. Specifically, removing the soft gating mechanism reduced switch detection accuracy from 78.02% to 73.58%, indicating its role in smoothing switching transitions. Removing the latency-compensated\n322\t\n323\tFigure 2: Ablation analysis of average switch latency. (∗∗∗ : pvalue < 0.001 vs. SAGE Full)\n324\tTable 2: Ablation study of the proposed SAGE on the spontaneous attention-switching dataset.\n325\tModel\n326\tSAGE (Full)\n327\t–w/o Soft Gating\n328\t–w/o Latency Alignment\n329\t–w/o Uncertainty Strategy\n330\t–w/o All\n331\t\n332\tSI-SDR (dB)\n333\t8.67\n334\t8.12\n335\t7.86\n336\t7.41\n337\t6.73\n338\t\n339\tSTOI (%)\n340\t88.24\n341\t87.35\n342\t86.02\n343\t85.18\n344\t83.80\n345\t\n346\tACC (%)\n347\t78.02\n348\t73.58\n349\t71.34\n350\t74.26\n351\t66.47\n352\t\n353\talignment module further decreased accuracy to 71.34%, confirming its importance in reducing the temporal mismatch between EEG responses and switching events for timely tracking. Lastly, removing the uncertainty-driven conservative strategy lowered accuracy to 74.26%, demonstrating its role in suppressing unreliable decisions during uncertain EEG conditions.\n354\tFigure. 2 illustrated the impact of each module on switch delay.\n355\tRemoving the latency alignment and soft gating mechanisms\n356\tincreased switch delay (2.58 s and 2.35 s, respectively), while\n357\tremoving the uncertainty-driven conservative strategy had a\n358\tsmaller effect on delay but significantly reduced speech extraction quality and robustness. Overall, the ablation results verified\n359\tthat each component plays a crucial role in improving speech\n360\tquality, reducing latency, and enhancing robustness in dynamic\n361\ttarget-speaker switching scenarios.\n362\t\n363\t5. Conclusion\n364\tIn this work, we propose SAGE, a switch-aware EEG-guided\n365\tsoft gating framework designed for dynamic target speaker extraction under in-trial attention switching in multi-speaker environments. SAGE combines a dual-stream speech separator\n366\twith a switch-aware soft gating module, leveraging adaptive\n367\ttemperature scaling to suppress artifacts and ensure smooth\n368\ttransitions during attention switches. It further incorporates\n369\tlatency-compensated alignment with learnable temporal shifts\n370\tto address label–switch latency gaps, and an uncertainty-driven\n371\tconservative strategy to stabilize decisions under noisy, unreliable EEG segments. Experimental results on our spontaneous\n372\tattention-switching dataset show that SAGE consistently outperforms baselines, achieving 8.67 dB SI-SDR and 88.24%\n373\tSTOI, while reducing the average switch latency to 2.04 s. Ablation studies confirm complementary gains from each component, improving both extraction quality and switching stability.\n374\tFuture work will explore cross-subject generalization and robustness under more diverse acoustic conditions.\n375\t\n376\t\f6. Acknowledgments\n377\tThis work was supported by the National Key Research\n378\tand Development Program of China (2025YFF0518003,\n379\t2023YFF1203502), the National Natural Science Foundation of\n380\tChina (62371217), and the Center for Computational Science\n381\tand Engineering at Southern University of Science and Technology.\n382\t\n383\t7. Generative AI Use Disclosure\n384\tAll (co-)authors are responsible and accountable for the work\n385\tand content of the paper, and consent to its submission. Generative AI tools were used for editing and polishing manuscripts,\n386\tbut were not used for producing a significant part of the\n387\tmanuscript.\n388\t\n389\t8. References\n390\t[1] S. Haykin and Z. Chen, “The cocktail party problem. neural computation,” Neural Computation, vol. 17, no. 9, pp. 1875–1902,\n391\t2005.\n392\t[2] B. Mirkovic, S. Debener, M. Jaeger, and M. De Vos, “Decoding\n393\tthe attended speech stream with multi-channel EEG: implications\n394\tfor online, daily-life applications,” Journal of Neural Engineering, vol. 12, no. 4, p. 046007, 2016.\n395\t[3] L. Straetmans, B. Holtze, S. Debener, M. Jaeger, and B. Mirkovic,\n396\t“Neural tracking to go: auditory attention decoding and saliency\n397\tdetection with mobile EEG,” Journal of Neural Engineering,\n398\tvol. 18, no. 6, p. 066054, 2021.\n399\t[4] M. Delcroix, K. Zmolikova, K. Kinoshita, A. Ogawa, and\n400\tT. Nakatani, “Single channel target speaker extraction and recognition with speaker beam,” in Proceedings of the International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n401\t(ICASSP), 2018, pp. 5554–5558.\n402\t[5] M. Ge, C. Xu, L. Wang, E. Chng, J. Dang, and H. Li, “L-spex:\n403\tLocalized target speaker extraction,” in Proceedings of the International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n404\t(ICASSP), 2022, pp. 7287–7291.\n405\t[6] C. Xu, W. Rao, E. Chng, and H. Li, “Spex: Multi-scale time\n406\tdomain speaker extraction network,” IEEE/ACM Transactions on\n407\tAudio, Speech, and Language Processing, vol. 18, pp. 1370–1384,\n408\t2020.\n409\t[7] Y. Luo and N. Mesgarani, “Conv-tasnet: Surpassing ideal\n410\ttime–frequency magnitude masking for speech separation,”\n411\tIEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing, vol. 27, no. 8, pp. 1256–1266, 2019.\n412\t[8] X. Ji, M. Yu, C. Zhang, D. Su, T. Yu, X. Liu, and D. Yu,\n413\t“Speaker-aware target speaker enhancement by jointly learning\n414\twith speaker embedding extraction,” in Proceedings of the International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n415\t(ICASSP), 2020, pp. 7294–7298.\n416\t[9] M. Ge, C. Xu, L. Wang, E. Chng, J. Dang, and H. Li, “Spex+: A\n417\tcomplete time domain speaker extraction network,” in Proceedings of the Interspeech, 2020, pp. 1406–1410.\n418\t[10] R. Gu, L. Chen, S. Zhang, J. Zheng, Y. Xu, M. Yu, D. Su, Y. Zou,\n419\tand D. Yu, “Neural spatial filter: Target speaker speech separation assisted with directional information,” in Proceedings of the\n420\tInterspeech, 2019, pp. 4290–4294.\n421\t[11] K. Ito, M. Yamamoto, and K. Nagamatsu, “Audio-visual speech\n422\tenhancement method conditioned in the lip motion and speakerdiscriminative embeddings,” in Proceedings of the International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n423\t(ICASSP), 2021, pp. 6668–6672.\n424\t[12] M. Hosseini, L. Celotti, and E. Plourde, “End-to-end brain-driven\n425\tspeech enhancement in multi-talker conditions,” IEEE/ACM\n426\tTransactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing,\n427\tvol. 30, pp. 1718–1733, 2022.\n428\t\n429\t[13] X. Wang, Y. Ding, L. Wang, and F. Chen, “Smr-awarenet: an\n430\tadaptive smr-aware neural network for EEG auditory attention\n431\tguided target speech extraction,” in Proceedings of the International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n432\t(ICASSP), 2025, pp. 1–5.\n433\t[14] Z. Pan, G. Wichern, F. G. Germain, S. Khurana, and J. Le Roux,\n434\t“NeuroHeed+: Improving neuro-steered speaker extraction with\n435\tjoint auditory attention detection,” in Proceedings of the International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n436\t(ICASSP), 2024, pp. 11 456–11 460.\n437\t[15] C. Fan, H. Zhang, W. Huang, J. Xue, J. Tao, J. Yi, Z. Lv, and\n438\tX. Wu, “DGSD: Dynamical graph self-distillation for EEG-based\n439\tauditory spatial attention detection,” Neural Networks, vol. 1, no.\n440\t179, p. 106580, 2024.\n441\t[16] W. Nogueira, G. Cosatti, I. Schierholz, M. Egger, B. Mirkovic,\n442\tand A. Büchner, “Toward decoding selective attention from\n443\tsingle-trial EEG data in cochlear implant users,” IEEE Transactions on Biomedical Engineering, vol. 67, no. 1, pp. 38–49, 2019.\n444\t[17] J. O’sullivan, A. Power, N. Mesgarani, S. Rajaram, J. Foxe,\n445\tB. Shinn-Cunningham, M. Slaney, S. Shamma, and E. Lalor, “Attentional selection in a cocktail party environment can be decoded\n446\tfrom single-trial EEG,” Cerebral Cortex, vol. 25, no. 7, pp. 1697–\n447\t1706, 2015.\n448\t[18] X. Wang, X. Chen, Y. Ding, Y. Ban, S. Yu, Y. Tsao, and F. Chen,\n449\t“Eeg and eye-tracking driven dynamic target speaker extraction\n450\twith spontaneous attention switching,” in Proceedings of the International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), 2026, pp. 6651–6655.\n451\t[19] J. Belo, M. Clerc, and D. Schön, “EEG-based auditory attention\n452\tdetection and its possible future applications for passive BCI,”\n453\tFrontiers in Computer Science, vol. 3, p. 661178, 2021.\n454\t[20] S. Geirnaert, S. Vandecappelle, E. Alickovic, A. De Cheveigne,\n455\tE. Lalor, B. Meyer, S. Miran, T. Francart, and A. Bertrand,\n456\t“Electroencephalography-based auditory attention decoding: Toward neurosteered hearing devices,” IEEE Signal Processing\n457\tMagazine, vol. 38, no. 4, pp. 89–102, 2021.\n458\t[21] J. O’Sullivan, Z. Chen, J. Herrero, G. McKhann, S. Sheth,\n459\tA. Mehta, and N. Mesgarani, “Neural decoding of attentional\n460\tselection in multi-speaker environments without access to clean\n461\tsources,” Journal of Neural Engineering, vol. 14, no. 5, p. 056001,\n462\t2017.\n463\t[22] S. Fuglsang, T. Dau, and J. Hjortkjær, “Noise-robust cortical\n464\ttracking of attended speech in real-world acoustic scenes,” NeuroImage, vol. 156, pp. 435–444, 2017.\n465\t[23] B. Somers, E. Verschueren, and T. Francart, “Neural tracking of\n466\tthe speech envelope in cochlear implant users,” Journal of Neural\n467\tEngineering, vol. 16, no. 1, p. 016003, 2019.\n468\t[24] X. Wang, Y. Ding, Y. Ban, L. Wang, and C. F., “An open noninvasive eeg dataset for spontaneous auditory attention switch decoding.” Scientific Data, 2026.\n469\t[25] J. Zhang, Q. Xu, Q. Zhu, and Z. Ling, “BASEN: Time-domain\n470\tbrain-assisted speech enhancement network with convolutional\n471\tcross attention in multi-talker conditions,” in Proceedings of the\n472\tInterspeech, 2023, pp. 3117–3121.\n473\t[26] D. De Silva, S. Cai, S. Pahuja, T. Schultz, and H. Li, “NeuroSpex+: Dual-task training of neuro-guided speaker extraction\n474\twith speech envelope and waveform,” in Proceedings of the Interspeech, 2025, pp. 5568–5572.\n475\t[27] C. Fan, Y. Chen, J. Zhou, Z. Pan, J. Zhang, Y. Gao, X. Yang,\n476\tZ. Wen, and Z. Lv, “M3ANet: Multi-scale and multi-modal alignment network for brain-assisted target speaker extraction,” in Proceedings of the International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI), 2025, pp. 8040–8048.\n477\t\n478\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "5dd30711-a7f6-4622-932c-c9cfbcacea35", "uuid": "2e3adec3-1268-4c7b-9dde-0ae50b4191c2", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T15:28:30.627Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/sage_paper.txt", "content": "SAGE: Switch-Aware EEG-Guided Soft Gating for Target Speaker Extraction\nwith In-Trial Switching\nXuefei Wang ID 1 , Ximin Chen ID 1 , Yuting Ding ID 1 , Chunlin Li2 , Fei Chen ID 1,∗∗\n1\n\nDepartment of Electronic and Electrical Engineering, Southern University of Science and\nTechnology, Shenzhen, China\n2\nSchool of Biomedical Engineering, Capital Medical University, Beijing, China\n12431243@mail.sustech.edu.cn, fchen@sustech.edu.cn\n\narXiv:2608.01623v1 [eess.SP] 3 Aug 2026\n\nAbstract\nEEG-guided target speaker extraction is challenging under intrial auditory attention switching, where neural noise and intrinsic latency can delay or destabilize attention tracking. Conventional methods struggle with dynamic switches and often cause\ndiscontinuities at switching points. Therefore, we propose\nSAGE, a switch-aware EEG-guided soft gating framework that\ntreats in-trial switching as dynamic selection. SAGE generates\ntwo candidate speech streams with a robust separator and uses\nan EEG-guided switch-aware gating module to produce smooth\nfusion weights and suppress transition artifacts. We further integrate latency-compensated alignment and an uncertainty-driven\nconservative strategy to handle latency discrepancies and fluctuating EEG reliability. SAGE outperforms baselines, achieving 8.67 dB SI-SDR and 88.24% STOI while reducing average\nswitching latency to 2.04 s. By coupling neural decoding with\nspeech separation, it enables robust target extraction in dynamic\nscenarios.\nIndex Terms: target speaker extraction, EEG decoding, switchaware gating, neural latency compensation, uncertainty-aware\nstrategy\n\n1. Introduction\nThe cocktail party problem [1] describes the remarkable human\nability to focus on a target talker in complex multi-speaker environments through selective auditory attention. Target speaker\nextraction (TSE) aims to address this challenge by leveraging\nauxiliary cues to separate the attended speech signal from a mixture. Electroencephalography (EEG) provides a direct measure\nof a listener’s neural activity and cognitive state, offering neurophysiological evidence of which speaker is being attended [2,3].\nWith millisecond-level temporal resolution, EEG can track the\ndynamics of attention in real time, making it an attractive physiological signal for guiding target speaker extraction.\nDriven by rapid advances in deep learning, the performance\nof TSE has improved substantially [4, 5]. Conventional TSE\nmethods typically rely on enrollment utterances of the target\nspeaker or pre-extracted speaker embeddings, requiring prior\naccess to target speech samples and performing well in fixed\nsingle-target settings [6–9]. To reduce dependence on enrollment speech, researchers have explored alternative auxiliary\ncues, including spatial information for localization-assisted separation [10], visual cues such as lip movements to enhance directional focus and robustness [11], and biologically inspired\nmechanisms that emulate neural processing to improve source\nseparation [12].\n** indicates the corresponding author.\n\nIn recent years, EEG-based TSE has emerged as an active research topic. By decoding auditory attention information\nfrom EEG, neuro-steered target speaker extraction has shown\nstrong potential for practical applications [13]. Pan et al. proposed NeuroHeed+, which significantly improves neuro-guided\nspeaker extraction by jointly modeling auditory attention decoding [14]. Fan et al. introduced DGSD, which applies\ndynamic-graph self-distillation to EEG-based auditory spatial\nattention decoding and improves the accuracy of feature learning [15]. Despite these advances, most existing EEG-guided\nTSE methods adopt a key simplifying assumption that the listener’s attentional focus remains static during an experimental\nsegment. This assumption overlooks a more realistic setting\nwhere listeners may spontaneously switch attention between\nspeakers within a single trial, referred to as in-trial switching.\nEEG-guided TSE under in-trial attention switching introduces two central challenges. First, EEG signals are inherently\nnoisy and non-stationary, and are sensitive to environmental\ninterference and inter-subject variability. These properties often degrade the reliability of neural decoding around switching moments, reducing the accuracy of detecting and tracking\nattention shifts [16–18]. Second, neural responses exhibit intrinsic latency, leading to inevitable latency discrepancies between attention-switch labels and true switches. When combined with conventional approaches that rely on fixed speaker\nreferences and lack mechanisms to accommodate dynamically\nswitching attention, this mismatch often produces delayed responses and audible discontinuities at switching points, which\nsubstantially harms continuity and intelligibility [19–21]. Although prior studies have attempted to improve EEG decoding\nthrough signal enhancement and feature selection [22, 23], they\ndo not provide dedicated mechanisms tailored to spontaneous\nin-trial attention switching, and cannot fundamentally address\nthe performance degradation caused by endogenous, dynamic\nattention switches.\nTo tackle these issues, we propose SAGE, a switch-aware\nEEG-guided soft-gating framework for target speaker extraction\nduring spontaneous in-trial speaker switching. SAGE makes\nthree main contributions: (1) it uses a front-end separation module to generate two candidate speech streams for dynamic selection; (2) it designs an EEG-guided switch-aware gating module with adaptive temperature scaling to produce smooth fusion\nweights and avoid abrupt switching artifacts; and (3) it incorporates latency-compensated alignment with learnable temporal\nshifts and an uncertainty-driven conservative strategy to handle EEG noise and neural latency. Experiments on spontaneous\nattention-switching datasets show that SAGE improves speech\nextraction performance, reduces switching delay, and enhances\nsystem stability compared with strong baselines.\n\n\fFigure 1: The overall framework of the proposed SAGE, switch-aware EEG-guided soft gating for target speaker extraction.\n\n2. Method\nIn this section, we propose a novel dynamic target speaker extraction method based on EEG signals, designed to overcome\nthe limitations of traditional speech separation methods in handling in-trial target speaker switching.\n2.1. Architecture\nAs shown in Fig. 1, the overall framework consists of two main\nparts: speech separation and EEG attention regulation. The\nEEG attention regulation module includes EEG feature extractor, switch-aware soft gating, uncertainty-driven conservative\nstrategy, and latency compensation alignment. The architecture\nutilizes EEG-guided attention switching information and compensates for neural delays, effectively ensuring smooth transitions during target speaker switching. Given a mixed speech\nwaveform Stmix ∈ RT ×1 and an EEG sequence e(t) ∈ RC\n(with C channels) sampled and synchronized to the audio timeline, the model outputs the extracted target waveform ŝ(t) that\nfollows the attended speaker even when attention switches.\nThe speech feature extraction module follows the ConvTasNet pipeline [7], which encodes the time-domain mixture\ninto a learnable latent representation and performs separation\nby estimating masks in the latent space. Given the input mixture Stmix , a 1-D convolutional encoder first maps it to an embedding sequence, followed by layer normalization:\n\u0010\n\u0010\n\u0011\u0011\nYt = LayerNorm Conv1Denc Stmix ,\n(1)\n′\n\nwhere Yt ∈ RT ×D denotes the encoded features, T ′ is the\nframe length after convolution, and D is the encoder feature dimension. On top of Yt , a temporal convolutional separator composed of stacked dilated 1-D convolutional blocks aggregates\nlong-range context and predicts two mask sequences through\na 1 × 1 projection with PReLU and sigmoid activations. The\nmasks are applied to the mixture embedding to form two candidate latent streams. Finally, a decoder reconstructs the timedomain candidates to produce two candidate speech streams,\n\ns1 (t), s2 (t), which provide input for speaker selection and synthesis.\nThe EEG attention regulation module generates a timevarying weight function g(t) based on the neural activity in the\nEEG signals, which controls the selection of the final output\nspeech stream. We employ a soft selection mechanism based\non a time window, aiming to predict the dynamic change of the\ntarget speaker based on the EEG signal. The process is formulated as:\nŝ(t) = g(t) · s1 (t) + (1 − g(t)) · s2 (t),\n\n(2)\n\nwhere g(t) ∈ [0, 1] is the dynamic weight generated by the\nneural network based on the EEG signal, indicating the selected\ntarget speaker at time t.\n2.2. Switch-aware Soft Gating\nEEG attention switches are often annotated as discrete change\npoints, yet the underlying neural evidence evolves continuously,\nexhibiting a slowly varying transition rather than an instantaneous jump. Direct hard selection often produces discontinuities or momentary leakage of both speakers around switching\npoints. Therefore, we propose a switch-aware soft gating mechanism that can introduce smoother transitions by incorporating\nswitch probabilities and temperature scaling.\nWe predict a switch probability psw (t) ∈ [0, 1] reflecting\nwhether attention is changing around time t, and an attention\nbias logit α(t) ∈ R indicating which candidate is more likely\nattended. Both are produced from the aligned EEG feature via\nlightweight temporal modeling which enforces temporal consistency. We first compute an intermediate temperature-controlled\ngate gtemp (t):\n\u0013\n\u0012\nα(t)\n, τ (t) = τ0 + λ · psw (t), (3)\ngtemp (t) = σ\nτ (t)\nwhere τ0 > 0 is a base temperature and λ ≥ 0 controls\nhow much additional smoothing is introduced near potential\n\n\fswitches. Although gtemp (t) already adapts its sharpness via\nτ (t), residual jitter can still arise from EEG noise, especially\nnear switching regions. We therefore obtain the final gating signal g(t) by applying a local diffusion to gtemp (t):\n\u0010\n\u0011\ng(t) = clip (gtemp ∗ wσ(t0 ) )(t) ,\n(4)\nwhere ∗ denotes 1D convolution over time, wσ(t0 ) is a normalized smoothing kernel centered at a detected switching center t0\nwith learnable width σ(t0 ) > 0, and clip(·) enforces the range\n[0, 1]. The switching centers {t0 } are obtained by thresholding\npeaks or local maxima in psw (t).\n2.3. Latency Compensation and Uncertainty Strategy\nIn practice, attention-related EEG patterns inevitably lag behind\nannotated switch events, exhibiting intrinsic latency discrepancies that vary significantly across subjects and trials. Without\nexplicitly compensating for such delays, the gating signal g(t)\ntends to update after the labeled switch time, increasing wrongspeaker leakage in the transition region. To alleviate this issue,\nwe introduce a differentiable time-alignment module that produces an aligned EEG representation e′ (t).\nRather than predicting a single global offset, we formulate\nalignment as a local, time-varying soft shift within a bounded\nwindow:\nD\nX\ne′ (t) =\nw∆ (t) · e(t − ∆),\n(5)\n∆=−D\n\nwhere D is the maximum shift, e(t) ∈ RC denotes the Cchannel EEG sample at time t and w∆ (t) is the time-shifting\nweight dynamically predicted by the network. This operation\ncan be interpreted as differentiable temporal resampling over a\nplausible neural-latency window, allowing the model to softly\naggregate EEG evidence from nearby time points instead of\ncommitting to a hard shift. The aligned feature e′ (t) is then\nfed into the gating and switch-prediction network to estimate\nα(t) and psw (t), better synchronizing EEG-driven decisions\nwith acoustic changes. However, EEG quality is not constant,\nartifacts, subject fatigue, and transient noise can make EEGderived cues unreliable. Over-confident gating under unreliable\nEEG can lead to persistent wrong-speaker selection. Therefore,\nWe also incorporate an uncertainty-driven conservative strategy that down-weights aggressive switching and encourages\nsmoother gating when EEG is uncertain. We estimate uncertainty by performing K stochastic forward passes with dropout\nenabled in the EEG-related subnetworks, producing a set of gatK\ning samples g (k) (t)k=1 . The uncertainty can be measured as the\ntemporal variance:\n\u0012n\noK \u0013\n(k)\nu(t) = V ar\ng (t)\n,\n(6)\nk=1\n\nand normalized into [0, 1] to obtain a stable control signal. A\nhigher u(t) indicates lower confidence in EEG-driven selection. When u(t) is high, we increase the preference for continuity by amplifying a smoothness penalty. We implement this\nby uncertainty-weighted smoothness regularization:\nX\nLsmooth =\nu(t) · R (g(t)) ,\n(7)\nt\n\nwhere R(g(t)) is a differentiable smoothness regularizer. This\ndesign prevents the model from making sharp, low-confidence\ndecisions, while still allowing fast transitions when EEG evidence is both strong and reliable.\n\n2.4. Loss Function Design\nDuring training, we optimize the model with a combination of\nspeech reconstruction loss, gating regularization, and switchstructure constraints to improve both extraction quality and temporal stability.\nTo suppress jitter in g(t), we design a switch-aware variant\nthat relaxes the penalty near predicted switching moments:\nX\nLtv-sw =\n(1 − βpsw (t)) , |g(t) − g(t − 1)|,\n(8)\nt\n\nwhere β ∈ [0, 1] controls how much smoothing is reduced when\npsw (t) is high. This encourages stable gating during steady\nattention while allowing faster transitions when the model detects a switch. Besides, We supervise the extracted waveform\nŝ(t) with the ground-truth target speech s(t). Since SI-SDR is\na maximization metric, we minimize its negative form. The\nfinal training objective combines these losses, including the\nuncertainty-weighted smoothness in Eq. (7), with appropriate\nweights:\nL = −SI-SDR (ŝ(t), s(t)) + γ1 Ltv-sw + γ2 Lsmooth ,\n\n(9)\n\nwhere γ1 , γ2 are scalar weights. To ensure stable convergence,\nwe adopt a tri-stage training strategy. In stage one, we train the\naudio separator using only audio supervision. In stage two, we\npartially freeze the separator and jointly train the EEG-related\nmodules. Finally, we perform end-to-end fine-tuning with a\nsmaller learning rate.\n\n3. Experiments\n3.1. Dataset\nIn this study, we use a custom-built dataset for spontaneous\nauditory attention switching based on electroencephalogram\n(EEG) signals [24]. The experimental procedures have been\napproved by the Ethical Review Board of Southern University\nof Science and Technology (Approval No. 2022DZX003). The\nparticipants consisted of 18 healthy native Mandarin-speaking\nadults (ages 18-27) with normal hearing. During the experiment, EEG data was recorded using 64 electrodes placed according to the international 10-20 system, with a sampling rate\nof 500 Hz and mastoid reference electrodes. The auditory\nstimuli consisted of spatialized mixed speech from one male\nand one female speakers, presented at +90◦ and −90◦ azimuths. Participants were instructed to switch attention spontaneously and signal the switch using a button press. The experiments were conducted in a soundproof room, and the stimuli\nwere presented through headphones at a sound pressure level\nof 65 dB SPL. Data preprocessing followed standard procedures, including artifact removal, re-referencing, EEG downsampling to 128 Hz, and application of a 0.1-45 Hz bandpass filter. Additional information and data can be found at:\nhttps://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17413336.\n3.2. Experimental Details\nThe proposed model was implemented in Python 3.9 using the\nPyTorch framework, and all experiments were conducted on a\nhigh-performance computing system equipped with NVIDIA\nV100 GPUs to accelerate training and evaluation. During training, we employed the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of\n1 × 10−4 , a batch size of 16, and default momentum parameters to optimize model stability and convergence. Each participant’s dataset was split into training, validation, and test sets in a\n\n\f8:1:1 ratio to ensure the model was trained, validated, and evaluated on separate data. The training process spanned multiple\nepochs, and early stopping was employed based on validation\nloss to prevent overfitting. Additionally, regularization techniques such as dropout and weight decay were applied during\ntraining to improve the model’s robustness. During the evaluation phase, we reported scale-invariant signal-to-distortion ratio\n(SI-SDR), short-time objective intelligibility (STOI), switch detection accuracy (ACC) and average switch latency (ASL).\n\n4. Results and Discussion\n4.1. Comparison with Baseline Methods\nTo evaluate our EEG-based target speaker extraction approach,\nwe compared it with several baseline models. As shown in Table 1, our method, SAGE, outperformed all baselines across all\nmetrics on the spontaneous attention-switching dataset.\nSAGE achieved 8.67 dB in SI-SDR, surpassing BASEN\n(4.02 dB), NeuroHeed (4.96 dB), NeuroSpex+ (6.21 dB), and\nM3ANet (7.13 dB), demonstrating better interference suppression and speech distortion reduction for higher reconstruction\nquality. In terms of speech intelligibility, SAGE reached a STOI\nscore of 88.24%, outperforming BASEN (74.83%), NeuroHeed\n(79.57%), NeuroSpex+ (82.84%), and M3ANet (84.30%), preserving perceptually important speech components for clearer,\nmore intelligible extraction. SAGE achieved the lowest average switching latency of 2.04 s, outperforming BASEN (2.93\ns), NeuroHeed (2.81 s), NeuroSpex+ (2.56 s), and M3ANet\n(2.37 s). This advantage underscored the effectiveness of the\nlatency-compensated alignment module in alleviating the temporal mismatch between EEG attention labels and actual auditory attention switching, enabling faster and more reliable tracking. Overall, the proposed SAGE framework delivered superior\nperformance over multiple strong baselines, with particularly\npronounced gains during dynamic target switching.\nTable 1: Comparison with representative EEG-based target\nspeaker extraction baselines on the spontaneous attentionswitching dataset.\nMethods\nBASEN [25]\nNeuroHeed [14]\nNeuroSpex+ [26]\nM3ANet [27]\nSAGE (proposed)\n\nSI SDR (dB)\n4.02\n4.96\n6.21\n7.13\n8.67\n\nSTOI (%)\n74.83\n79.57\n82.84\n84.30\n88.24\n\nASL (s)\n2.93\n2.81\n2.56\n2.37\n2.04\n\n4.2. Ablation Study\nTo evaluate the contributions of each component in the proposed\nmethod, we conducted ablation experiments by removing the\nswitch-aware soft gating, latency-compensated alignment, and\nuncertainty-driven conservative strategy modules, as well as a\nvariant where all modules were removed.\nTable 2 presented the performance of each configuration in\nterms of SI-SDR, STOI, and switch detection accuracy. Removing any module resulted in performance degradation, highlighting the importance of each design choice. Specifically, removing the soft gating mechanism reduced switch detection accuracy from 78.02% to 73.58%, indicating its role in smoothing switching transitions. Removing the latency-compensated\n\nFigure 2: Ablation analysis of average switch latency. (∗∗∗ : pvalue < 0.001 vs. SAGE Full)\nTable 2: Ablation study of the proposed SAGE on the spontaneous attention-switching dataset.\nModel\nSAGE (Full)\n–w/o Soft Gating\n–w/o Latency Alignment\n–w/o Uncertainty Strategy\n–w/o All\n\nSI-SDR (dB)\n8.67\n8.12\n7.86\n7.41\n6.73\n\nSTOI (%)\n88.24\n87.35\n86.02\n85.18\n83.80\n\nACC (%)\n78.02\n73.58\n71.34\n74.26\n66.47\n\nalignment module further decreased accuracy to 71.34%, confirming its importance in reducing the temporal mismatch between EEG responses and switching events for timely tracking. Lastly, removing the uncertainty-driven conservative strategy lowered accuracy to 74.26%, demonstrating its role in suppressing unreliable decisions during uncertain EEG conditions.\nFigure. 2 illustrated the impact of each module on switch delay.\nRemoving the latency alignment and soft gating mechanisms\nincreased switch delay (2.58 s and 2.35 s, respectively), while\nremoving the uncertainty-driven conservative strategy had a\nsmaller effect on delay but significantly reduced speech extraction quality and robustness. Overall, the ablation results verified\nthat each component plays a crucial role in improving speech\nquality, reducing latency, and enhancing robustness in dynamic\ntarget-speaker switching scenarios.\n\n5. Conclusion\nIn this work, we propose SAGE, a switch-aware EEG-guided\nsoft gating framework designed for dynamic target speaker extraction under in-trial attention switching in multi-speaker environments. SAGE combines a dual-stream speech separator\nwith a switch-aware soft gating module, leveraging adaptive\ntemperature scaling to suppress artifacts and ensure smooth\ntransitions during attention switches. It further incorporates\nlatency-compensated alignment with learnable temporal shifts\nto address label–switch latency gaps, and an uncertainty-driven\nconservative strategy to stabilize decisions under noisy, unreliable EEG segments. Experimental results on our spontaneous\nattention-switching dataset show that SAGE consistently outperforms baselines, achieving 8.67 dB SI-SDR and 88.24%\nSTOI, while reducing the average switch latency to 2.04 s. Ablation studies confirm complementary gains from each component, improving both extraction quality and switching stability.\nFuture work will explore cross-subject generalization and robustness under more diverse acoustic conditions.\n\n\f6. Acknowledgments\nThis work was supported by the National Key Research\nand Development Program of China (2025YFF0518003,\n2023YFF1203502), the National Natural Science Foundation of\nChina (62371217), and the Center for Computational Science\nand Engineering at Southern University of Science and Technology.\n\n7. Generative AI Use Disclosure\nAll (co-)authors are responsible and accountable for the work\nand content of the paper, and consent to its submission. Generative AI tools were used for editing and polishing manuscripts,\nbut were not used for producing a significant part of the\nmanuscript.\n\n8. References\n[1] S. Haykin and Z. Chen, “The cocktail party problem. neural computation,” Neural Computation, vol. 17, no. 9, pp. 1875–1902,\n2005.\n[2] B. Mirkovic, S. Debener, M. Jaeger, and M. De Vos, “Decoding\nthe attended speech stream with multi-channel EEG: implications\nfor online, daily-life applications,” Journal of Neural Engineering, vol. 12, no. 4, p. 046007, 2016.\n[3] L. Straetmans, B. Holtze, S. Debener, M. Jaeger, and B. Mirkovic,\n“Neural tracking to go: auditory attention decoding and saliency\ndetection with mobile EEG,” Journal of Neural Engineering,\nvol. 18, no. 6, p. 066054, 2021.\n[4] M. Delcroix, K. Zmolikova, K. Kinoshita, A. Ogawa, and\nT. Nakatani, “Single channel target speaker extraction and recognition with speaker beam,” in Proceedings of the International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n(ICASSP), 2018, pp. 5554–5558.\n[5] M. Ge, C. Xu, L. Wang, E. Chng, J. Dang, and H. Li, “L-spex:\nLocalized target speaker extraction,” in Proceedings of the International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n(ICASSP), 2022, pp. 7287–7291.\n[6] C. Xu, W. Rao, E. Chng, and H. Li, “Spex: Multi-scale time\ndomain speaker extraction network,” IEEE/ACM Transactions on\nAudio, Speech, and Language Processing, vol. 18, pp. 1370–1384,\n2020.\n[7] Y. Luo and N. Mesgarani, “Conv-tasnet: Surpassing ideal\ntime–frequency magnitude masking for speech separation,”\nIEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing, vol. 27, no. 8, pp. 1256–1266, 2019.\n[8] X. Ji, M. Yu, C. Zhang, D. Su, T. Yu, X. Liu, and D. Yu,\n“Speaker-aware target speaker enhancement by jointly learning\nwith speaker embedding extraction,” in Proceedings of the International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n(ICASSP), 2020, pp. 7294–7298.\n[9] M. Ge, C. Xu, L. Wang, E. Chng, J. Dang, and H. Li, “Spex+: A\ncomplete time domain speaker extraction network,” in Proceedings of the Interspeech, 2020, pp. 1406–1410.\n[10] R. Gu, L. Chen, S. Zhang, J. Zheng, Y. Xu, M. Yu, D. Su, Y. Zou,\nand D. Yu, “Neural spatial filter: Target speaker speech separation assisted with directional information,” in Proceedings of the\nInterspeech, 2019, pp. 4290–4294.\n[11] K. Ito, M. Yamamoto, and K. Nagamatsu, “Audio-visual speech\nenhancement method conditioned in the lip motion and speakerdiscriminative embeddings,” in Proceedings of the International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n(ICASSP), 2021, pp. 6668–6672.\n[12] M. Hosseini, L. Celotti, and E. Plourde, “End-to-end brain-driven\nspeech enhancement in multi-talker conditions,” IEEE/ACM\nTransactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing,\nvol. 30, pp. 1718–1733, 2022.\n\n[13] X. Wang, Y. Ding, L. Wang, and F. Chen, “Smr-awarenet: an\nadaptive smr-aware neural network for EEG auditory attention\nguided target speech extraction,” in Proceedings of the International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n(ICASSP), 2025, pp. 1–5.\n[14] Z. Pan, G. Wichern, F. G. Germain, S. Khurana, and J. Le Roux,\n“NeuroHeed+: Improving neuro-steered speaker extraction with\njoint auditory attention detection,” in Proceedings of the International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n(ICASSP), 2024, pp. 11 456–11 460.\n[15] C. Fan, H. Zhang, W. Huang, J. Xue, J. Tao, J. Yi, Z. Lv, and\nX. Wu, “DGSD: Dynamical graph self-distillation for EEG-based\nauditory spatial attention detection,” Neural Networks, vol. 1, no.\n179, p. 106580, 2024.\n[16] W. Nogueira, G. Cosatti, I. Schierholz, M. Egger, B. Mirkovic,\nand A. Büchner, “Toward decoding selective attention from\nsingle-trial EEG data in cochlear implant users,” IEEE Transactions on Biomedical Engineering, vol. 67, no. 1, pp. 38–49, 2019.\n[17] J. O’sullivan, A. Power, N. Mesgarani, S. Rajaram, J. Foxe,\nB. Shinn-Cunningham, M. Slaney, S. Shamma, and E. Lalor, “Attentional selection in a cocktail party environment can be decoded\nfrom single-trial EEG,” Cerebral Cortex, vol. 25, no. 7, pp. 1697–\n1706, 2015.\n[18] X. Wang, X. Chen, Y. Ding, Y. Ban, S. Yu, Y. Tsao, and F. Chen,\n“Eeg and eye-tracking driven dynamic target speaker extraction\nwith spontaneous attention switching,” in Proceedings of the International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), 2026, pp. 6651–6655.\n[19] J. Belo, M. Clerc, and D. Schön, “EEG-based auditory attention\ndetection and its possible future applications for passive BCI,”\nFrontiers in Computer Science, vol. 3, p. 661178, 2021.\n[20] S. Geirnaert, S. Vandecappelle, E. Alickovic, A. De Cheveigne,\nE. Lalor, B. Meyer, S. Miran, T. Francart, and A. Bertrand,\n“Electroencephalography-based auditory attention decoding: Toward neurosteered hearing devices,” IEEE Signal Processing\nMagazine, vol. 38, no. 4, pp. 89–102, 2021.\n[21] J. O’Sullivan, Z. Chen, J. Herrero, G. McKhann, S. Sheth,\nA. Mehta, and N. Mesgarani, “Neural decoding of attentional\nselection in multi-speaker environments without access to clean\nsources,” Journal of Neural Engineering, vol. 14, no. 5, p. 056001,\n2017.\n[22] S. Fuglsang, T. Dau, and J. Hjortkjær, “Noise-robust cortical\ntracking of attended speech in real-world acoustic scenes,” NeuroImage, vol. 156, pp. 435–444, 2017.\n[23] B. Somers, E. Verschueren, and T. Francart, “Neural tracking of\nthe speech envelope in cochlear implant users,” Journal of Neural\nEngineering, vol. 16, no. 1, p. 016003, 2019.\n[24] X. Wang, Y. Ding, Y. Ban, L. Wang, and C. F., “An open noninvasive eeg dataset for spontaneous auditory attention switch decoding.” Scientific Data, 2026.\n[25] J. Zhang, Q. Xu, Q. Zhu, and Z. Ling, “BASEN: Time-domain\nbrain-assisted speech enhancement network with convolutional\ncross attention in multi-talker conditions,” in Proceedings of the\nInterspeech, 2023, pp. 3117–3121.\n[26] D. De Silva, S. Cai, S. Pahuja, T. Schultz, and H. Li, “NeuroSpex+: Dual-task training of neuro-guided speaker extraction\nwith speech envelope and waveform,” in Proceedings of the Interspeech, 2025, pp. 5568–5572.\n[27] C. Fan, Y. Chen, J. Zhou, Z. Pan, J. Zhang, Y. Gao, X. Yang,\nZ. Wen, and Z. Lv, “M3ANet: Multi-scale and multi-modal alignment network for brain-assisted target speaker extraction,” in Proceedings of the International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI), 2025, pp. 8040–8048.\n\n\f", "numLines": 478, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 478}}}
